  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_CHAPTER5_DIAGRAM_Q02 - Pedigree chart analysis of Mendelian disorder</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>Study the given pedigree chart of a human family for a Mendelian disorder, where shaded symbols represent affected individuals. Which mode of inheritance is depicted by this pedigree?</p>
      <p>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 460 270" width="460" height="270" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background: #ffffff;">
          <rect x="10" y="10" width="440" height="250" rx="8" fill="#fafafa" stroke="#cccccc" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          
          <!-- Generation labels -->
          <text x="28" y="55" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#333333">I</text>
          <text x="28" y="135" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#333333">II</text>
          <text x="28" y="215" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#333333">III</text>

          <!-- Gen I: Unaffected Male (160, 50) + Unaffected Female (240, 50) -->
          <rect x="148" y="38" width="24" height="24" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="172" y1="50" x2="228" y2="50" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <circle cx="240" cy="50" r="12" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>

          <!-- Descent line Gen I to Gen II -->
          <line x1="200" y1="50" x2="200" y2="90" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="80" y1="90" x2="360" y2="90" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="80" y1="90" x2="80" y2="118" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="150" y1="90" x2="150" y2="118" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="250" y1="90" x2="250" y2="118" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="310" y1="90" x2="310" y2="118" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="360" y1="90" x2="360" y2="118" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>

          <!-- Gen II individuals -->
          <!-- II-1 Unaffected Male -->
          <rect x="68" y="118" width="24" height="24" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- II-2 Affected Male -->
          <rect x="138" y="118" width="24" height="24" fill="#d32f2f" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- II-3 Unaffected Male (husband) -->
          <rect x="188" y="118" width="24" height="24" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="212" y1="130" x2="238" y2="130" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- II-4 Unaffected Female -->
          <circle cx="250" cy="130" r="12" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- II-5 Affected Female -->
          <circle cx="310" cy="130" r="12" fill="#d32f2f" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- II-6 Unaffected Male -->
          <rect x="348" y="118" width="24" height="24" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>

          <!-- Descent line Gen II (II-3 x II-4) to Gen III -->
          <line x1="225" y1="130" x2="225" y2="168" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="165" y1="168" x2="285" y2="168" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="165" y1="168" x2="165" y2="195" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="225" y1="168" x2="225" y2="195" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="285" y1="168" x2="285" y2="195" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>

          <!-- Gen III individuals -->
          <!-- III-1 Affected Male -->
          <rect x="153" y="195" width="24" height="24" fill="#d32f2f" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- III-2 Unaffected Female -->
          <circle cx="225" cy="207" r="12" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- III-3 Unaffected Female -->
          <circle cx="285" cy="207" r="12" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/>

          <!-- Legend -->
          <rect x="340" y="185" width="14" height="14" fill="#d32f2f" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text x="360" y="197" font-size="11" fill="#333333">Affected</text>
          <rect x="340" y="207" width="14" height="14" fill="#d9e2ec" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text x="360" y="219" font-size="11" fill="#333333">Unaffected</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The pedigree spans three generations (I, II, and III). In generation I, both parents are unaffected, yet they have both an affected son and an affected daughter in generation II. In generation II, an unaffected daughter marries an unaffected male, and they produce an affected son in generation III.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Diagram Interpretation</strong><br/>Standard pedigree symbols are used: squares represent males, circles represent females, unshaded symbols represent unaffected individuals, and shaded symbols represent affected individuals.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Formulae and Principles</strong><br/>In a dominant trait, at least one parent of an affected child must express the trait. In a recessive trait, two unaffected heterozygous (carrier) parents can produce affected offspring. In an X-linked recessive trait, an affected daughter must inherit the mutant X chromosome from her father as well as her mother, meaning her father must be affected. In a Y-linked trait, only males are affected and transmission is strictly father-to-son.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>First, because unaffected parents in generation I produce affected children in generation II (and similarly in generation II to III), the trait skips generations and cannot be dominant (ruling out autosomal dominant and X-linked dominant). Second, because a female in generation II is affected, the trait cannot be Y-linked. Third, because the affected female in generation II has an unaffected father in generation I, the trait cannot be X-linked recessive (otherwise her father would be affected). Therefore, both parents in generation I must be heterozygous carriers for an autosomal recessive allele.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Assigning \(Aa\) to both parents in generation I allows \(aa\) affected sons and daughters in generation II, and an \(Aa \times Aa\) marriage in generation II can produce an \(aa\) affected son in generation III. The final answer is an autosomal recessive trait.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
