      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The diagram shows the DNA triplet, mRNA codon, and amino acid sequence (positions \(5\), \(6\), and \(7\)) of the \(\beta\)-globin chain of haemoglobin. In the normal \(Hb^A\) allele, the mRNA codon at position \(6\) is \(\text{GAG}\), which codes for Glutamic acid (\(\text{Glu}\)). In the sickle-cell \(Hb^S\) allele, the mRNA codon at position \(6\) is \(\text{GUG}\), which codes for amino acid \(X\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Diagram Interpretation</strong><br/>Position \(5\) (\(\text{Pro}\)) and position \(7\) (\(\text{Glu}\)) remain unchanged between the normal \(Hb^A\) and mutant \(Hb^S\) peptides, indicating that the reading frame downstream of position \(6\) is not shifted.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Formulae and Principles</strong><br/>A change in a single base pair of DNA is called a point mutation. By contrast, deletions or insertions of base pairs cause frameshift mutations that alter all subsequent codons, and chromosomal aberrations involve loss or gain of large DNA segments.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>Comparing the two alleles at the sixth codon shows a single base substitution from \(\text{GAG}\) to \(\text{GUG}\) in the mRNA (corresponding to \(\text{A} \to \text{T}\) in the DNA coding strand). Because only a single base pair is replaced, this is a point mutation. In sickle-cell anaemia, the codon \(\text{GUG}\) at the sixth position of the \(\beta\)-globin chain incorporates Valine (\(\text{Val}\)) in place of Glutamic acid (\(\text{Glu}\)). Therefore, amino acid \(X\) is Valine.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>The mutant haemoglobin molecule undergoes polymerisation under low oxygen tension, changing the shape of red blood cells from a biconcave disc to an elongated sickle-like structure. Thus, \(X\) is Valine and the condition is caused by a point mutation.</p>
